.TH RULERTOO 1 "October 2026" "rulertoo" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rulertoo \- on-screen X11 ruler with real physical dimensions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rulertoo
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rulertoo
is a borderless on-screen ruler for X11. It sizes itself using the
physical millimetre dimensions reported by XRandR for the monitor the
ruler sits on, rather than a single global DPI value.
.PP
The window is an override-redirect overlay: it stays above normal
clients and is not constrained by the window manager when moved or
resized (including over a desktop panel).
.PP
Marks follow classic metric spacing (1 mm / 5 mm / 10 mm). Segments of
the window that hang onto another output, or into the gap between
monitors, are left blank with only the outline drawn; the scale is
valid on one monitor at a time.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-length " " \fIMM\fR
Initial physical length in millimetres (default: 300; range 20–2000).
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\-height " " \fIPX\fR
Thickness of the ruler in pixels (default: 52; range 28–200).
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-alpha " " \fIA\fR
Background opacity from 0.0 to 1.0 for schemes that use transparency
(default: 0.40). Fully opaque schemes ignore this for the panel fill.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-unit " " \fIUNIT\fR
Numbering unit:
.BR cm ,
.BR mm ,
.BR in ,
or
.BR px
(default:
.BR cm ).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-vertical
Start in vertical orientation (default is horizontal); same as
.BR "\-\-rotation 90" .
.TP
.BR \-\-rotation " " \fIDEG\fR
Start rotated clockwise by
.BR 0 ", " 90 ", " 180 " or " 270
degrees. 0 numbers left to right, 90 top to bottom, 180 right to left,
270 bottom to top. Labels always stay upright.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version string and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help message and exit.
.SH CONTROLS
.TP
Left-drag on the body
Move the ruler.
.TP
Drag the free-end handle
Resize the length. The handle is at the end opposite the zero mark
(right or bottom, left or top when rotated 180 or 270 degrees); the
zero edge stays put. Length is continuous so motion maps one-to-one to
pixels.
.TP
Middle-drag
Shift the numbering origin along the ruler.
.TP
.BR r " or the " R " button (middle of the ruler)"
Rotate 90 degrees clockwise: 0 → 90 → 180 → 270 → 0. The ruler turns
around the clicked point, or for
.B r
around the pointer when it is over the ruler (otherwise the centre),
so the spot under the pointer stays there. The window is then clamped
onto the current monitor when it fits.
Right-clicking the resize handle also rotates.
.TP
.BR f " or the " F " button"
Flip the numbering direction in place (0 ↔ 180, 90 ↔ 270), again
keeping the spot under the pointer.
.TP
.B u
Cycle units: cm → mm → in → px → cm.
.TP
.B c
Cycle colour schemes: default → opaque → inverted → highcontrast →
amber → default.
.TP
.B Shift-C
Start calibration (30 cm target; see
.B CALIBRATION
).
.TP
.B Ctrl-C
While calibrating, cycle the reference length (5/10/20/30 cm, 2/4/8/12 in).
.TP
Right-click outside the handle, or
.BR Esc " / " q
Quit. Settings are written to the config file on exit.
.SH CALIBRATION
When EDID or XRandR millimetre sizes are wrong, press
.B Shift-C
to enter calibration mode (starts at a 30 cm target):
.IP \(bu 2
A popup next to the ruler shows the active target length, the axis
and the output being calibrated.
.IP \(bu 2
Resize the window until it matches a physical object of that length.
.IP \(bu 2
Press
.B Ctrl-C
to cycle targets:
5 cm, 10 cm, 20 cm, 30 cm, then 2 in, 4 in, 8 in, 12 in
(smaller lengths suit panels that cannot fit 30 cm).
.IP \(bu 2
Press
.B Enter
to store a per-output scale factor, or
.B Esc
to cancel.
.PP
Each axis is calibrated separately: a horizontal ruler calibrates the
screen's horizontal axis, a vertical ruler the vertical one. Rotate with
.B r
and calibrate again to correct both.
Rotation is disabled while calibrating.
.PP
Factors are saved as
.BI cal. OUTPUT = W , H
in the config file, one per native panel axis (EDID width and height),
and applied whenever that XRandR output is used
(\fItrue_mm\fR = \fIedid_mm\fR × \fIfactor\fR).
A single value (older configs) applies to both axes.
.SH COLOUR SCHEMES
.TP
.B default
Semi-transparent cool grey panel with black marks (original look).
.TP
.B opaque
Solid light panel, no see-through.
.TP
.B inverted
Dark panel with light marks.
.TP
.B highcontrast
Black panel with yellow marks.
.TP
.B amber
Warm semi-transparent panel.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rulertoo/config
.TQ
.I ~/.config/rulertoo/config
Persisted state written on exit (and when a calibration is confirmed):
window position, orientation, unit, length, thickness, alpha, colour
scheme, and per-monitor calibration factors. Command-line options
override the matching values for that run only.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DISPLAY
X11 display to use.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory for the config file (default:
.IR ~/.config ).
.SH NOTES
The scale follows the monitor under the ruler (via XRandR). Crossing
onto another output blanks the overhang until the scale monitor
updates. Override-redirect means the window is not listed like a
normal application frame; click the ruler to raise it and give it
keyboard focus.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xrandr (1),
.BR X (7)
